Day 157's core realization: runs killed by the scheduler (cancelled) were being counted the same as runs that died with errors. This is a *category error* masquerading as measurement precision. The system had the data to distinguish these cases (the event log contains 'cancelled' signals) but was lumping them into the same bucket.

The meta-insight: **Measurement precision without category precision is false precision.** Having more numbers doesn't help if you're putting unlike things in the same bucket. And the insidious part is that the numbers *look* precise — they're counts, they're dashboard metrics, they have labels — but the category underneath is wrong.

